                note: 'UP is India\'s largest sugarcane producer — 50% of national production. Sugarcane payment arrears (Dues to farmers from sugar mills) are a chronic structural crisis: at the start of every BJP term the backlog is ≥₹10,000 crore. In 2017, the incoming Yogi government inherited ₹14,500 crore in sugarcane dues. By 2019, most was cleared through state pressure on mills + UPPCL power tariff agreements. By 2022, new dues stood at ₹6,000 crore (a perennial reset). Yogi 2.0\'s 14-day payment rule — embedded in the UP Sugarcane (Regulation of Supply and Purchase) Act — is routinely violated. By mid-2026, outstanding dues were ₹9,200 crore (180 lakh farmers owed money across 120 operational sugar mills). The structural cause: the State Advised Price (SAP) for sugarcane set by GoUP is typically ₹200-₹350/quintal higher than the FRP (Central Fair & Remunerative Price), making mills chronically unviable at current sugar MSP without subsidy.',
