        parameter: `Employment entitlement delivery, financial management and fraud prevention`,
        finding: `Only 2–4% of MGNREGS-demanding households in Karnataka received the full statutory entitlement of 100 days of employment during 2019-24; the majority received only 1–30 days. State delays in releasing funds to the State Employment Guarantee Fund incurred ₹36.26 crore in penal interest, while simultaneously ₹32.78 crore was recorded as inadmissible expenditure under the administrative head. Unemployment allowances of ₹1.28 crore legally due to workers were not paid. Fraudulent transactions were found in housing and Solid Waste Management works.`,
        actual: `Only 2–4% of demanding households received full 100-day entitlement; ₹36.26 Cr penal interest on delayed state releases; ₹32.78 Cr inadmissible administrative expenditure; ₹1.28 Cr unemployment allowances unpaid; fraud in housing and SWM works.`,
        reportExcerpt: `"2 per cent to 4 per cent households were provided employment for 100 days… State Employment Guarantee Fund entailed a penal interest of ₹36.26 crore… Audit also observed inadmissible expenditure of ₹32.78 crore under the administrative head of the Scheme… Fraudulent transactions under housing works such as payment for houses already completed, payment to ineligible beneficiaries [were found]."`,
