        parameter: `FHTC functionality in arid districts, asset quality, water pressure and sustainability of GP-level management`,
        finding: `Rajasthan — India's largest state and the driest — received ₹28,400 crore under JJM to provide tap water connections to 1.08 crore rural households. CAG found that 44% of certified Functional Household Tap Connections in arid districts (Barmer, Jaisalmer, Jodhpur, Bikaner, Nagaur) were non-functional at the time of audit — connections existed on paper but had no water supply. 38% of connections in sampled districts had inadequate water pressure (below the prescribed 7 litres per person per day supply at the tap). ₹2,840 crore in capital expenditure had no Utilisation Certificates from Gram Panchayats. Groundwater dependency (45% of village water schemes rely on Over-Exploited aquifers) means the JJM connections will fail permanently as aquifer levels drop over the next decade unless surface water supplement arrives.`,
