        parameter: `Tribal district health infrastructure, ASHA incentive payments, YSR health card scheme overlap and IMR in newly-formed districts`,
        finding: `AP's NHM programme faces a dual challenge: the inherited YSR Aarogyasri health insurance scheme (covering 92% of AP families for secondary and tertiary care) has crowded out investment in primary health infrastructure. CAG found PHC doctor vacancy at 34% in the 5 newly formed tribal districts, with sub-health centre ANM vacancy at 48%. ₹420 crore in NHM grants lapsed unspent in 2022-23, as AP's focus on cashless hospitalisation de-prioritised prevention and PHC strengthening. ASHA incentive payment delays averaged 3.8 months — causing outreach work to decline in Alluri Sitharama Raju, Parvathipuram Manyam and Visakhapatnam agency areas. The tribal belt IMR in Paderu and Araku areas was 44 per 1,000 against the AP state average of 26.`,
        actual: `PHC doctor vacancy 34% in 5 tribal districts; ANM vacancy 48%; ₹420 Cr lapsed; ASHA payment delay 3.8 months; tribal belt IMR 44 vs state avg 26; YSR Aarogyasri coverage 92% for hospitalisation.`,
