        scheme: `Kaleswaram Lift Irrigation Scheme — Godavari`,
        schemeSlug: `jal-jeevan`,
        reportRef: `Report No. 02 of 2024`,
        reportYear: 2024,
        severity: 'critical',
        parameter: `Project cost, structural safety of barrages, irrigation benefit vs investment, debt sustainability`,
        finding: `The Kaleswaram Multi-Purpose Lift Irrigation Scheme — described by BRS as "the world's largest lift irrigation project" — was sanctioned at ₹80,500 crore in 2018 and had reached ₹1,32,000 crore by 2024, a 64% escalation. CAG found that the Medigadda barrage (the project's foundation structure, on the Godavari at the AP-Telangana border) developed pier sinking — pillars subsiding 17 cm into the riverbed — making it structurally unsafe. The barrage was shut down in October 2023 after the discovery. Approximately ₹1,12,000 crore was spent against an irrigation benefit to only 6.1 lakh acres (target: 45 lakh acres — only 13.5% of the targeted benefit achieved at the time of shutdown). The Irrigation Development Corporation borrowed ₹80,000 crore through off-balance-sheet mechanisms, bypassing FRBM limits.`,
        actual: `Cost escalated ₹80,500 Cr → ₹1,32,000 Cr (64% overrun); Medigadda barrage pier sinking 17 cm — shut down Oct 2023; only 6.1 lakh acres benefited vs 45 lakh target (13.5%); ₹80,000 Cr off-balance-sheet TSIDCL debt.`,
        reportExcerpt: `"Project cost escalated from ₹80,500 crore to ₹1,32,000 crore — a 64 per cent overrun… the Medigadda barrage developed pier sinking of 17 cm — pillars subsiding into the riverbed — and was shut down in October 2023 on structural safety grounds… only 6.1 lakh acres received irrigation benefit against the 45 lakh acre target — 13.5 per cent of promised benefit… ₹80,000 crore was borrowed through off-balance-sheet TSIDCL mechanisms, bypassing FRBM limits."`,
