        finding: `Assam's NHM programme confronts India's most severe annual flood disruption to health delivery — the Brahmaputra floods (June-October) inundate 20-30% of Assam's PHCs each year, making health access impossible for 4-5 million flood-affected residents for 3-4 months. CAG found that 38% of PHCs in 12 flood-prone districts (Dhubri, South Salmara, Barpeta, Nagaon, Morigaon, Hojai, Golaghat, Majuli, Jorhat, Sivasagar, Dibrugarh, Tinsukia) lacked flood-resilient building structures and had no emergency drug stockpiles for flood isolation periods. Japanese Encephalitis (JE) — prevalent in the pig-farming and Brahmaputra floodplain belt — recorded 580 cases and 64 deaths in 2023-24, with vaccination coverage reaching only 68% of target population in Upper Assam districts. Tea garden worker health — 7.5 lakh workers and 14 lakh dependents living in plantation labour lines — is managed by plantation hospitals that CAG found operating at 42% doctor availability against the Plantation Labour Act's requirements.`,
