                status: 'partial',
                note: 'KIIFB — established by the LDF government in 2016 — is Kerala\'s off-budget infrastructure financing vehicle: it borrows from domestic and international capital markets (Masala bonds — rupee-denominated bonds listed on London Stock Exchange — and NHB/HUDCO loans) and funds Kerala\'s road, hospital, school and university infrastructure, repaid from Kerala\'s tax receipts over 15-20 years. By 2021 KIIFB had sanctioned ₹56,000 crore across 958 projects — the largest off-budget infrastructure commitment by any Indian state in proportion to GSDP. Key KIIFB achievements (2016-21): 1,500 km of roads widened and resurfaced (Life Mission road grid); 47 hospitals constructed or upgraded; 4,341 school classrooms built under the "School Management Committee" programme; Thiruvananthapuram Smart City infrastructure (₹1,800 crore). CAG Report on KIIFB (2022): found ₹8,200 crore in sanctioned projects without DPR (Detailed Project Reports); 18% of disbursed funds "unverified in deployment"; KIIFB\'s total contingent liability (₹56,000 crore) constitutes 28% of Kerala\'s GSDP and is not reflected in the state\'s fiscal deficit calculation — a significant off-balance-sheet liability. The CAG noted KIIFB circumvents FRBM debt ceilings, creating hidden public debt. The controversy became central to Kerala\'s dispute with the Finance Commission and GoI over fiscal space.',
