        finding: `Goa's iron ore mining — stopped by Supreme Court order in 2012 (for violation of environmental clearances) and re-authorised by the Goa Mineral Development (Removal of Difficulties) Act 2014 and subsequent MMDR amendments — has been a governance disaster. CAG found ₹3,400 crore in unpaid royalties and cess from 88 mining leases that operated after the SC ban using "inventoried ore" justifications but in fact extracted fresh ore. The 2018 fresh SC ban (based on illegal renewals) and the 2020 MMDR Act amendment allowing fresh auctioning has resulted in zero new mines operational by 2024 — creating a ₹2,800 crore annual economic vacuum. CAG found the state government's auction process favoured a single entity's lease aggregation in the Bicholim-Satari belt, violating competitive bidding norms.`,
