                status: 'partial',
                note: 'Manipur\'s HIV prevalence of 1.15% (India\'s 2nd highest after Mizoram) is driven by IV drug use on the "Golden Triangle" transit route — Myanmar heroin crosses into India at Moreh (Tengnoupal), passes through Manipur to supply NE markets. The 2017-22 government expanded JNIMS (Jawaharlal Nehru Institute of Medical Sciences, Imphal) capacity and created 6 new district-level De-Addiction Centres. Poppy eradication (2,80,000+ acres destroyed) reduced opium cultivation supply, pushing addicts to cheaper methamphetamine (Yaba tablets from Myanmar). CAG Performance Audit on Drinking Water & Public Health (Manipur, 2019-20) found: 68% of Primary Health Centres in hill districts lacked 24×7 service; 52% of medical officer posts in hill PHCs vacant; 78% of PHC drug indents met within time — better than national average but still below need. The 2023 ethnic conflict then collapsed hill district health infrastructure further.',
                noteHi: 'MN HIV: 1.15% — देश में दूसरा। JNIMS क्षमता विस्तार; 6 नए जिला De-Addiction केंद्र। अफीम उन्मूलन → मेथांफेटामाइन में बदलाव। CAG D&PH: पहाड़ी PHC में 52% चिकित्सा अधिकारी रिक्त; 68% PHC 24×7 सेवा से वंचित।',
                cagVerdict: 'CAG Performance Audit on Drinking Water & Public Health Manipur (2019-20): 68% of hill PHCs lacking 24×7 service; 52% of MO posts in hill PHCs vacant; 78% drug indents met within time (better than national avg); 1.15% HIV prevalence (2nd India) driven by IV drug use on Golden Triangle corridor — ART programme reaching only 68% of eligible patients.',
