                status: 'partial',
                note: 'Manipur\'s water supply challenge is acute: the valley (Imphal area) relies on Kangchup-Chingarel water treatment plant, while hill districts depend on gravity-fed spring water — unreliable in dry season. JJM Manipur progress: 9.8 lakh of 12.6 lakh rural households connected to tap water (77.8% coverage, as of June 2026). This is above the NE India average (72%) but below the national average (80.3%). Hill district coverage (Churachandpur: 61%, Tengnoupal: 52%, Kangpokpi: 64%) is significantly below valley coverage (Imphal West: 91%, Imphal East: 88%). The 2023 ethnic conflict severely disrupted JJM work in hill districts — 48 JJM construction sites abandoned. Waterborne disease burden: diarrhoea, cholera and typhoid in hill districts is 3-4x the valley rate. Prior to JJM, Manipur\'s tribal communities depended on streams that pass through jhum (slash-and-burn) fields — leading to high coliform contamination.',
