                status: 'partial',
                note: 'The NPP-led MDA (Meghalaya Democratic Alliance) government replaced the Congress which had ruled Meghalaya almost continuously since 1972 — a half-century of single-party dominance that had created deep patronage networks. Conrad Sangma\'s NPP (2018-23) introduced Meghalaya Government e-Marketplace (GeM adoption for state procurement), launched single-window project clearance, and activated the Meghalaya Lokayukta — which under Congress had rarely investigated sitting ministers. Notable achievements: introduction of biometric attendance for government employees (reducing ghost-employee salaries); e-tendering for all contracts above ₹5 lakh (reducing contractor cartelisation). However, the most significant governance failure of the 2018-23 term was illegal coal mining. Despite the NGT\'s 2014 ban, the Meghalaya Mineral Development Corporation — under NPP\'s own MDA government — failed to shut a single active illegal mine. The Ksan mine tragedy (December 2018 — 8 months into NPP\'s term) killed 15 miners in a mine that had been flagged by the SC\'s monitoring committee and ignored. Meghalaya\'s governance reform was partial: administrative modernisation improved, but the coal-mining political economy (operators funding all parties) remained unchanged.',
