                status: 'not-fulfilled',
                note: 'The 2018 NPP manifesto acknowledged the NGT coal mining ban (2014) as a livelihood crisis for East and West Jaintia Hills. Conrad Sangma petitioned the Centre and the SC monitoring committee for a "regulated extraction" framework under Sixth Schedule tribal land rights — arguing that tribal land owners have constitutional authority over their subsoil minerals that cannot be overridden by the NGT order. The SC rejected this argument in 2019, upholding the NGT ban. The NPP government then proposed a Coal Mine Rehabilitation Mission (2020) — ₹500 crore for skills, MSME development and eco-tourism in the mining belt — which was approved by the Cabinet but received only ₹68 crore from GoI, and the state contributed ₹12 crore. Actual beneficiaries of alternative livelihood programmes: 4,200 out of 1.5 lakh affected. The Ksan mine tragedy (December 13, 2018) — 15 miners trapped 300 feet underground in a flooded rat-hole mine, irrecoverable — became the defining image of the NPP\'s failure to address the coal crisis. The mine had been reported to the state government and SC monitoring committee as illegal and active; no action was taken.',
                noteHi: 'NPP ने SC में Sixth Schedule तर्क दिया — 2019 में खारिज। Coal Mine Rehabilitation Mission: ₹500 करोड़ प्रस्ताव; ₹80 करोड़ मिले; 4,200 लाभार्थी (1.5 लाख में से)। Ksan (दिसंबर 2018): 15 मृत — NPP कार्यकाल की सबसे बड़ी विफलता।',
                cagVerdict: 'CAG Coal Mining Meghalaya 2025: 1,240 active illegal rat-hole mines despite NGT ban; Ksan mine repeatedly flagged by SC monitoring committee — political protection prevented closure; 60% East Jaintia families coal-dependent with no effective alternative livelihood programme.',
