                promiseHi: 'मेघालय को विश्वस्तरीय पर्यटन गंतव्य बनाना; Dawki, Cherrapुnji और जीवित जड़ पुलों को UNESCO धरोहर',
                status: 'partial',
                note: 'The NPP 2018 government inherited a largely untapped tourism potential and transformed Meghalaya\'s global visibility significantly. Key achievements: (1) Dawki\'s transparent Umngot river — bordering Bangladesh at Shnongpdeng — became India\'s most-photographed eco-tourism spot after a viral 2019 video showed tourists in glass-bottom boats on crystal-clear water. Dawki tourist footfall: 80,000 (2018) → 4.5 lakh (2023). (2) The Double-Decker Living Root Bridge at Nongriat (near Cherrapunji — a 3,000+ step descent into a gorge, creating living bridges from the aerial roots of Ficus elastica rubber trees — trained over generations by Khasi communities) was listed in India\'s UNESCO Tentative List in 2021 for "living traditions". (3) Shillong branded as India\'s "Rock Capital" — hosting Shillong Chamber Choir (BBC Cardiff world music finalists) and NE India\'s largest music festival. Tourist arrivals grew from 8 lakh (2018) to 12 lakh (2023). However, rapid growth at Dawki created plastic waste and congestion — requiring the government to introduce carrying-capacity restrictions (500 visitors/day) in 2022 — largely effective. The UNESCO nomination for living root bridges remains pending as of 2026.',
                noteHi: 'Dawki: 80,000 (2018) → 4.5 लाख (2023)। Double-Decker Root Bridge: UNESCO Tentative List 2021। शिलांग "Rock Capital"। कुल पर्यटक: 8 लाख → 12 लाख। Dawki carrying capacity: 500/दिन (2022)। UNESCO नामांकन 2026 तक लंबित।',
