                status: 'partial',
                note: 'Meghalaya\'s water supply challenge is paradoxical — the state has among India\'s highest rainfall (Mawsynram, Cherrapunji — the world\'s wettest places) yet 62% of rural households lacked safe piped water due to terrain and scattered settlements. JJM Meghalaya progress: tap connections rose from 12% (2019, among India\'s lowest) to 48% (2023) to 68% (mid-2026) — still below national 75% average. Key challenges: gravity-fed schemes efficient but require extensive pipeline over hilly terrain; 38% of water sources show seasonal dry-up; Garo Hills groundwater has fluoride issues. Umiam Lake (Shillong\'s primary drinking water reservoir, 220 sq km catchment): CAG Meghalaya Environment 2023 found 228 illegal structures in the buffer zone; nitrogen loading from Ri-Bhoi poultry farms at 3× safe limits. NPP government demolished 140 of 228 illegal structures by mid-2026.',
