                note: 'When MNF took power in December 2018, Mizoram\'s fiscal situation was already stressed: state debt of ₹15,800 crore (debt-GSDP ~38%), salary+pension consuming 48% of revenue receipts, own tax revenue only ₹480 crore (5.8% of receipts). The Zoramthanga-led MNF government (2018-23) fiscal record: (1) introduced biometric attendance for all state employees — reducing ghost-employee wage payments (saving an estimated ₹80 crore/year); (2) rationalised 220 redundant government offices; (3) grew excise revenue from ₹45 crore to ₹120 crore (2018-23) by regularising the partial liquor trade (Mizoram has historically alternated between prohibition and partial liquor trade — MNF under church pressure maintained partial prohibition but allowed licensed outlets for non-Mizo/non-Christian communities). By 2023 handover, state debt had grown to ₹19,400 crore despite these measures — reflecting COVID spending and MGNREGS obligations. The structural dependency on Central transfers (89%) barely moved. This is a structural constraint, not a governance failure — Mizoram\'s small population, limited natural resources and landlocked geography make own-revenue growth fundamentally difficult.',
