                note: 'Nagaland was one of the first states to receive AFSPA in 1958 — making it also the state with the longest unbroken AFSPA imposition in Indian history (67 years). AFSPA gives Army and Central Armed Police Forces (CAPF) the power to arrest, search, and use force without warrant in "disturbed areas". The Oting firing incident (December 4, 2021) — where an Army patrol ambushed a truck in Mon district (Konyak Naga territory) killing 13 civilians (coal miners returning home) and then firing on villagers who came to help (killing 1 more Army soldier in the confusion) — was Nagaland\'s worst civilian-Army incident since 2000, and triggered nationwide outrage and calls for AFSPA repeal. CM Neiphiu Rio (who leads the government as NDPP head) demanded immediate AFSPA repeal. GoI responded by not renewing AFSPA for 15 Nagaland police stations (April 2022) — the first partial reduction in Nagaland in decades. As of mid-2026, AFSPA remains in force in 8 of 16 Nagaland districts. Full withdrawal — the electoral promise — has not been achieved.',
