                note: 'BJP\'s 2018 Tripura win (35 seats, 43.6% vote share) — ending 25 years of CPI(M) rule — was among independent India\'s most significant state electoral upsets. The campaign centred on ending the Left\'s alleged political violence against BJP-RSS workers (100+ alleged BJP worker murders were cited). After the win, Biplab Kumar Deb (CM 2018-22) faced immediate controversy: a mob demolished a Lenin statue in Belonia (March 5, 2018 — within days of result). Deb was replaced by Manik Saha in May 2022 after internal party revolt over his erratic leadership (he had claimed Alexander the Great visited Tripura and that internet and satellite phones existed in the Mahabharata era, drawing national ridicule). Political violence largely ended after 2018. However the CAG Compliance Audit Tripura (2024) found that post-election relief scheme implementation (houses, roads, electricity for opposition-held areas) showed communal/political selectivity in beneficiary identification — a pattern that replicated some of the Left\'s own patronage-distribution methods. The 2023 Tripura election (Manik Saha-led BJP won 32/60 seats — diminished majority) suggests the anti-incumbency cycle continues despite political change.',
